      "pins": ["D2.1", "D2.2", "J3.2", "J8.2", "C1.1", "C2.1", "C3.1", "C4.1"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "servo power rail (D2/J3 pin2/J8 pin2/C1-C4)"],
      "observed": "On the right-hand side of the sheet, D2 (PMEG3030EP, '3A 360mV') is drawn vertically with its cathode bar on the top terminal, which is tied to the +5V global net, and its anode on the bottom terminal, which feeds the servo power rail (the node that runs to J3 pin 2, J8 pin 2 and the C1-C4 bank). This orients the Schottky so that it blocks current from +5V into the servo rail.",
      "expected": "The specification states D2 is the 'Diode from +5V into the servo power rail (feeding J3/J8 pin 2 and the C1-C4 bank)', i.e. anode on the +5V side and cathode on the servo-rail side so that +5V forward-biases the diode and powers the servos.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "D2 PMEG3030EP 3A 360mV - cathode (bar) at the +5V arrow, anode at the servo-rail node feeding J3 pin2/J8 pin2 and C1-C4 (sheet m5-pantilt, right column)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "D2 | PMEG3030EP Schottky ... Diode from `+5V` into the servo power rail (feeding J3/J8 pin 2 and the C1-C4 bank)"}
      ],
      "impact": "With the Schottky reversed, the +5V rail cannot forward-bias D2, so the dedicated servo power rail (J3/J8 pin 2 and C1-C4) is never energised from +5V. The pan/tilt servos would receive no supply in either PWM or Serial mode, and the bulk-capacitor inrush buffering described in the spec would be defeated.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Most of the schematic matches the specification (rail values, jumper network R9-R14, MAX3485/Q2 direction control, battery path Q1/SW1/R5, and all passive values). The one deviation supported by the drawing is D2, whose cathode is tied to +5V and anode to the servo rail, opposite to the spec's 'diode from +5V into the servo power rail', which would leave the servos unpowered."
}

